The isolated smoke is useful negative evidence: exposing the policy and tools without any selection is not an activation result.

Most positive routes are already captured here, so I would add one reproducible behavioral gate before closure rather than another schema-availability check:

- deterministic host tests proving the policy defaults off, is injected only for the actual active LCM engine, appears exactly once per turn across the tool loop, preserves the system/tool cache prefix and clean transcript/LCM ingest, and leaves compressor, other-engine, disabled, and older-host paths byte-identical and nonfatal;
- a profile/install matrix covering default, named-profile, profile-scoped `HERMES_HOME`, and custom-home installs, with destinations preflighted before writes, conflict refusal, idempotency, and rollback on partial failure;
- schema-derived validation of exact arguments, enums, and required fields in the skill and references, not only tool names;
- a fixed policy-OFF versus policy-ON activation matrix using identical model/settings and seeded evidence, retaining exact tool-call names, arguments, order, and results, plus unrelated and already-visible controls that must make no retrieval call.

Route assertions should be result-sensitive rather than forced chains: exact current-session evidence absent from active context uses `lcm_grep`, with `lcm_expand(store_id=...)` only when the snippet is insufficient; `lcm_describe` is conditional on a summary-node hit before `lcm_expand_query`; cross-conversation recall follows the `lcm_recall` result’s `expand_hint`; and hard time bounds use raw-message `lcm_grep(time_from/time_to)`.

One host edge also needs an explicit decision: current Hermes user-context composition skips plugin context for non-string or multimodal user messages, so either test a compatible path or narrow the “every prompt build” claim to supported text turns.

